What does the `no_early_error` property in this RFS module mean, and exactly when is a violation reported?

```rfs
module startup {
  enum State { Boot, Run, Error }
  initial Boot
  event Ready
  event Fault
  transition Boot on Ready {
    next Run;
  }
  transition Boot on Fault {
    next Error;
  }
  transition Run on Fault {
    next Error;
  }
  prop no_early_error : always[4] (state != Error) ;
}
```

How does RFS check this property at runtime? Walk through what the monitor does if Req occurs and Ack never comes.

```rfs
module ack_window {
  enum State { Idle, Pending }
  initial Idle
  event Req
  event SendAck
  output Ack
  transition Idle on Req {
    next Pending;
  }
  transition Pending on SendAck {
    next Idle;
    emit Ack;
  }
  prop req_acked : always (input_is(Req) -> bounded_eventually[3] (output_has(Ack))) ;
}
```

Which of these properties does RFS compile to an online monitor, and which are skipped?

```rfs
module subset_check {
  enum State { A, B }
  initial A
  event Go
  transition A on Go {
    next B;
  }
  prop ok_obligation : always (input_is(Go) -> bounded_eventually[3] (state == B)) ;
  prop bad_not_be : !bounded_eventually[3] (state == B) ;
  prop bad_next_be : next (bounded_eventually[3] (state == A)) ;
  prop bad_nested_always : always (always[2] (state == A)) ;
}
```

Why do these two RFS liveness properties fail to compile?

```rfs
module ev_noparen {
  enum State { Idle, Done }
  initial Idle
  event Go
  transition Idle on Go {
    next Done;
  }
  prop p : eventually state == Done ;
}
```

```rfs
module ev_nonbool {
  enum State { A }
  initial A
  event E
  var flag: bool init false
  var x: bit<8> init 0
  transition A on E { next A; }
  prop p : eventually (x) ;
}
```

This RFS spec validates fine, but the model checker skips one of its properties at codegen time - why?

```rfs
module nested {
  enum State { Idle, Done }
  initial Idle
  event Go
  transition Idle on Go { next Done; }
  transition Done on Go { next Done; }
  prop p : always (eventually (state == Done)) ;
}
```
